WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.141
Input handling is one of the most
important trust boundaries in a Go application.

2
00:00:04.221 --> 00:00:07.755
HTTP parameters, JSON bodies, form values,
headers, cookies, CLI arguments,

3
00:00:07.835 --> 00:00:11.155
files, environment variables, message
queue events, database records,

4
00:00:11.235 --> 00:00:14.769
and data from other services can all be
missing, malformed, stale, oversized,

5
00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:16.723
or inappropriate for the current action.

6
00:00:16.803 --> 00:00:18.463
A safe pattern is to parse before use.

7
00:00:18.543 --> 00:00:21.702
Code should define expected formats,
allowed values, length limits,

8
00:00:21.782 --> 00:00:24.781
numeric bounds, array sizes, content
types, and required fields.

9
00:00:24.861 --> 00:00:27.966
Parsing should happen close to the
boundary, before business logic,

10
00:00:28.046 --> 00:00:29.867
database operations, file operations,

11
00:00:29.947 --> 00:00:32.410
authorization decisions that depend on
request data,

12
00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:33.989
or calls to downstream services.

13
00:00:34.069 --> 00:00:35.676
Validation has more than one layer.

14
00:00:35.756 --> 00:00:38.754
Shape validation asks whether the data is
present and well formed.

15
00:00:38.834 --> 00:00:42.047
Meaning validation asks whether the value
is allowed for the workflow.

16
00:00:42.127 --> 00:00:45.822
Permission validation asks whether the
current caller may use that value for this

17
00:00:45.902 --> 00:00:46.277
action.

18
00:00:46.357 --> 00:00:48.177
A path, account ID, tenant ID, file name,

19
00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:51.845
or status value may have a valid format
and still be wrong for the current caller.

20
00:00:51.925 --> 00:00:53.156
Normalization needs care.

21
00:00:53.236 --> 00:00:56.503
Trimming whitespace, changing case,
decoding values, cleaning paths,

22
00:00:56.583 --> 00:01:00.438
parsing dates, and translating encodings
should be done consistently so validation

23
00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:02.606
and later use agree about what the value
means.

24
00:01:02.686 --> 00:01:06.167
For files and paths, code should be
especially clear about base directories,

25
00:01:06.247 --> 00:01:08.603
allowed names, and which operations are
permitted.

26
00:01:08.683 --> 00:01:10.878
Rejection behavior should also be
predictable.

27
00:01:10.958 --> 00:01:12.939
Callers need safe, understandable errors,

28
00:01:13.019 --> 00:01:16.714
while logs should help operators
investigate without recording sensitive input

29
00:01:16.794 --> 00:01:17.544
unnecessarily.

30
00:01:17.624 --> 00:01:21.211
Consistent validation makes failures
easier to handle and reduces surprising

31
00:01:21.291 --> 00:01:23.540
behavior between endpoints, jobs, and
services.
